module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.receipt_subject_surface_outlives_its_own_production_time

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data receipt_subject_surface_outlives_its_own_production_time: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "receipt_subject_surface_outlives_its_own_production_time" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a receipt whose validity is denominated in the RAW CONTENT of a high-traffic closure has an expected lifetime SHORTER THAN ITS PRODUCTION TIME, so the wall it feeds is unreachable in practice and is therefore never exercised** (INVALID STATE: an admission gate is pinned to a digest ranging over the closure CONTENT of many entries, and producing the evidence that satisfies it takes hours while the surface it ranges over is edited in minutes. Every dispatch is invalidated before it returns. Nothing is silently wrong -- the gate REFUSES correctly, every time -- and that is precisely the harm: A WALL THAT CAN NEVER BE SATISFIED IS INDISTINGUISHABLE, IN THE LEDGER, FROM A WALL THAT HOLDS, while contributing no evidence about the property it guards. Its RED is permanent and its GREEN is unreachable, so it stops discriminating. MEASURED, NOT IMAGINED: on gunbc#9725 the wet-lane receipt cost 2h31m to produce and was pinned to a semantic-subject digest over the closures of eighteen routed self-host entries plus the wet-route module. Between the dispatch tree and the first run that reached a verdict, files INSIDE that closure moved, landed by UNRELATED pull requests, none of them the receipt's own author, and A SINGLE AFTERNOON'S ARRIVALS WERE ALREADY SUFFICIENT. THE COUNT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CARRIED HERE, and its absence is part of the finding: the first attempt to state it was taken from a file-level diff, overstated, and was corrected against the measured closure WITHIN THE SAME EVENING, before this row had even landed -- a transcribed measurement is unreachable from the thing that produced it, so it rots while nobody touches either end. Worse, the severity moved OPPOSITE to the count: fewer arriving files means a SMALLER surface was already enough, so a reader anchored on the number would have read the correction as good news. NAME THE INSTRUMENT INSTEAD, and re-derive rather than quote: enumerate the closure with `v1_compiler.bin.claim_batch` `--print-entry-closure` over the routed entries -- a non-evaluating mode that prints the population the RESOLVER retained, not an import re-walk -- and intersect it against the files changed since the dispatch tree. Run it beside a positive control whose membership is known, because an empty intersection and a dead instrument print the same thing. The gate refused with `subject-mismatch axis=semantic-subject`, the run was otherwise clean at 3596 planned, 3596 executed, 0 claims failed and 0 unexpected failures, and the receipt was dead. WHY THE OBVIOUS REMEDIES ARE NOT REMEDIES, both of which were proposed and priced before being rejected: FREEZING the surface asks an entire fleet to stop touching the most-trafficked module in the repository for the duration of one receipt, which purchases one landing by spending every other lane's night -- and the surface is high-traffic FOR THE SAME REASON it is in every closure, so the two halves of the problem are one fact. WAITING FOR A QUIET PERIOD does not fix the mechanism, it lowers the arrival rate until a dispatch gets lucky, and getting lucky is not a property of the mechanism -- WAITING IS THE TREADMILL RUN SLOWER. And WAIVING the axis to land the artifact would admit it by disabling the property it exists to establish; where a NEIGHBOURING axis is already waived under a declared drop, that waiver was sound ONLY BECAUSE this axis still held, so waiving both leaves not a weakened guarantee but none. RECOGNITION RULE: for any gate pinned to a digest, compute two quantities and compare them -- the TIME TO PRODUCE the evidence, and the MEAN TIME BETWEEN EDITS to the surface the digest ranges over. If production time exceeds the edit interval, the gate is unreachable and every refusal it emits is a scheduling artefact rather than a finding about the subject. The tell is a refusal whose remedy is `re-run it and hope`, and a ledger that reads as a held wall while its green has never once been observed. THE ROOT IS THE SUBJECT SURFACE, NOT THE SCHEDULE: a digest that ranges over raw file bytes makes every edit inside the closure -- including edits that change no semantics, per DESIGN section 4c -- move the subject, so the lifetime is denominated in the WRONG UNIT. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation is a freeze nobody can grant. CEILING: structurally guaranteed -- a subject computed from what the routed entries semantically depend on cannot be moved by an edit those entries do not depend on. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability and not an artifact: a receipt subject that ranges over the SEMANTIC DEPENDENCE of the routed entries -- the annotation-erased projection of exactly the declarations they reach -- rather than over the bytes of every file in their closure, so that the surface shrinks to what the evidence is actually about and the expected lifetime exceeds the production time by construction. NOT satisfied by a faster dispatch, a quieter window, or a wider waiver, each of which leaves the denominator unchanged.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
